\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 How much do you know about Manus the animation github project created by 3Blue1Brown\
Just a quick note on the name: the project is called Manim (not "Manus") \'97 short for Math Animation. Here's what I know about it:\
What it is: Manim is an animation engine for explanatory math videos, used to create precise animations programmatically. It was created by Grant Sanderson, the person behind the YouTube channel 3Blue1Brown.\
Origin: This repository began as a personal project by the author of 3Blue1Brown for the purpose of animating those videos. The video-specific code (the actual scenes from his videos) is available in a separate 3b1b/videos repository.\
Two versions exist: In 2020, a group of developers forked it into what is now the community edition (ManimCE), with a goal of being more stable, better tested, quicker to respond to community contributions, and friendlier to get started with. The original repo is now called ManimGL (uses OpenGL for rendering), while the fork is maintained at ManimCommunity/manim.\
Tech stack: Manim runs on Python 3.6 or higher, and system requirements include FFmpeg, OpenGL, and LaTeX (optional, for rendering math equations).\
Popularity: The 3b1b/manim repo has around 86,900 stars and 7,300 forks on GitHub.\
Interactive workflow: Running manimgl with the -se flag drops you into an interactive mode at a specific line of a file, like a debugger, with an iPython terminal that can be used to interact with the scene.\
Video code: The 3b1b/videos repo contains the code used to generate the explanatory math videos found on 3Blue1Brown, though older projects may have code dependent on older versions of manim and may not run out of the box.\
In short, Manim is a powerful Python library for making beautiful math animations programmatically, and it's the engine behind all of 3Blue1Brown's iconic visuals. Were you looking to use it for something specific?\
\
